{number: 162}
{title: And Can It Be?}
{key: G}

{start_of_verse: 1}
And [G] can it be that [C] I should [G] gain
An interest in the [D7] Savior's [G] blood?
Died He for me, who [C] caused His [G] pain?
For me, who Him to [D7] death pur-[G] sued?
A-[D] mazing [G] love! how [C] can it [G] be
That Thou, my [C] God, shouldst [G] die [D7] for [G] me?
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 2}
'Tis [G] myst'ry all! The Im-[C] mortal [G] dies:
Who can explore His [D7] strange de-[G] sign?
In vain the firstborn [C] seraph [G] tries
To sound the depths of [D7] love di-[G] vine.
'Tis [D] mercy [G] all! Let [C] earth a-[G] dore,
Let angel [C] minds in-[G] quire [D7] no [G] more.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 3}
He [G] left His Father's [C] throne a-[G] bove—
So free, so infinite [D7] His [G] grace!
Emptied Himself of [C] all but [G] love,
And bled for Adam's [D7] helpless [G] race.
'Tis [D] mercy [G] all, im-[C] mense and [G] free;
For, O my [C] God, it [G] found [D7] out [G] me.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 4}
Long [G] my imprisoned [C] spirit [G] lay
Fast bound in sin and [D7] nature's [G] night;
Thine eye diffused a [C] quick'ning [G] ray,
I woke, the dungeon [D7] flamed with [G] light;
My [D] chains fell [G] off, my [C] heart was [G] free,
I rose, went [C] forth, and [G] fol-[D7] lowed [G] Thee.
{end_of_verse}
